===== docs/native-mods.md =====
# Native mods

`native_explosion_entities(dimension, center, entities)` (server) runs after `native_explosion_detonate`, before an explosion at `center` hurts and pushes anything: `entities` holds the ids of the entities in its reach (`ServerExplosion.hurtEntities`), players aside, which `native_living_incoming_damage` hears. Take out the ids to spare: they are neither hurt nor pushed, as NeoForge's `ExplosionEvent.Detonate` does with its entity list. Bundles need "hooks": 644.

`native_chat_suggestion_applied(original, start, end, suggestion, input)` reports a suggestion accepted in the open chat by Tab or a mouse click, after the text and caret have changed. `original` is the suggestion list's input before any completion; `start` and `end` are the replaced range in that string, in Java UTF-16 units (end exclusive). They keep referring to `original` when Tab cycles through the list. `suggestion` is the selected replacement text, and `input` is the actual resulting field value, including any length-limit truncation. This is a read-only notification: previews, list navigation, server replies, ordinary typing and command-block editors do not fire it. Trait mods implement `Hooks::chat_suggestion_applied`; mixin bundles use `chat_suggestion_applied`, hook version 1. Bundles need "hooks": 566 (assigned at merge).

API 362 adds `native_compute_fov_modifier(player, base, modifier)` on the client each tick for the local player's FOV scale, after vanilla effects and before zoom and camera easing. `player` contains the calculation inputs and `base` is the original scale. Non-finite modifiers or values outside 0.01..=10 fall back to `base`. It does not affect a spectator camera on a mob. Bundles need "hooks": 362.

The client hook `native_client_packet(packet)` hears each play packet the server sends, decoded (`ClientboundPlayPacket`, `horizon_protocol`'s `play::Clientbound`), just before the game handles it, custom payloads included and in the order they came. It only reads: the game handles the packet as it would without the mod. Packets the network thread handles itself (keep-alives, bundle delimiters, resource packs, cookies, server links, transfers, disconnects, the configuration switch, `minecraft:brand`, far terrain tiles) and the configuration phase are not heard. Bundles need "hooks": 408.

`native_registry_name(registry, id)` (`host.registry_name`) is the name of a protocol id in a registry: `minecraft:item`, `minecraft:data_component_type`, `minecraft:entity_type`, `minecraft:sound_event`, `minecraft:particle_type` and the like. It names what `native_client_packet`'s packets carry as numbers (a `Stack`'s item and components, `AddEntity`'s type, `Sound`, `LevelParticles`). On a client the ids are those of the server it is connected to, so a registry the server synced (enchantments, painting variants) and a mod's own data components, when the server carries them, name too; on a server they are the ids it sends. It answers `None` for an unknown registry, an id out of range or negative, and while no server is connected. Bundles need `"hooks": 637`.

The client hook `native_render_name_tag(entity_id, kind, vanilla_visible, visible, text)` runs at player and mob, armor stand, and item frame name decisions before shared geometry. Set `visible` to override vanilla and `text` to `Some` literal replacement text (NeoForge `RenderNameTagEvent`). Distance and entity availability still apply; the local player's tag is considered only when its own camera and settings gates allow it. Bundles need "hooks": 372.

Native mods are **mixin bundles** that change the game's own Rust source before it is compiled, with
[horizon-mixins](https://github.com/horizon-rust/horizon-mixins) (MIT, by levzzz5154, made for Horizon). A bundle
is a `horizon-mixin.toml` and a few `.rs` helpers that hook into named functions of the game (HEAD, BEFORE_CALL,
RETURN, MODIFY_ARG, OVERWRITE...; the tool's README lists them). A port such as Veinminer is three HEAD hooks and
one module. Start a mod from [horizon-native-mod-template](https://github.com/horizon-rust/horizon-native-mod-template).

Native mods are native code with the player's rights, not sandboxed. They are always on in the launcher: there is no
switch. The first install shows a trust notice, and every build waits behind a red warning; the registry lists one only
after a review (its later releases are checked by machine); and they play in a game of their own, built for the
instance they are installed in.

## Compilation cache

Native builds share `native/target/fast` (Performance and Dev) and `native/target/release`.
After a build finishes, the launcher clears these compiler outputs if their combined size exceeds
30 GiB. The installed game or exported server stays usable; a later changed build recompiles.
`horizon-launcher settings native-cache-gib N` changes the limit (`default` restores 30; `0`
disables automatic cleanup). This is an after-build limit, not a cap on peak compilation space.

`horizon-launcher native-cache` reports its path, size in bytes and limit as JSON.
`horizon-launcher native-cache --clear` frees the compilation cache immediately when builds are idle.
Both use the same process lock as native builds; cleanup also refuses busy Cargo profile locks.
It preserves lock files, refuses symbolic links and Windows junctions, and never targets installed
versions, exported servers, instances, saves, mods, Rust toolchains or downloaded crates.


## Conditional item model properties

A client mod supplies its own namespaced `minecraft:condition` property by implementing
`Hooks::item_model_property`. This hook is the registration: match your property name, answer
through `value`, and leave other names untouched. The resolver calls it when choosing a branch,
not when loading resources. It defaults to false without a provider and skips dispatch entirely
when no trait mods are loaded. Vanilla properties do not enter the hook.

```rust,ignore
fn item_model_property(
    &self,
    _host: &dyn Host,
    event: &mut ItemModelPropertyEvent<'_>,
) {
    if event.property == "example:charged" {
        event.value = event.stack.patch.get("example:charged").and_then(Option::as_ref)
            .and_then(serde_json::Value::as_bool) == Some(true);
    }
}
```

`Host`, `ItemModelPropertyEvent`, and `StackView` are exported by `horizon_client::mod_api`. The stack is borrowed,
including for nested bundle items: `item`, `count`, explicit component `patch` (not merged
defaults), and the runtime bundle selection. Listeners run in mod order; later answers win.
Use `"property": "example:charged"` with both `on_true` and `on_false` in the item's
`minecraft:condition` node. Custom special model renderers are not supplied by this hook.
Bundles need `"hooks": 385` once this pending hook is stamped.

## Item frame rendering

`native_render_item_in_frame(entity_id, pos, stack, canceled)` runs before a visible item frame draws its item or map, including glow and invisible frames. Set `canceled` to hide the item without hiding the frame. Bundles need "hooks": 361.

## Villager farming

Farmers recognize mod blocks made from farmland and `CropBlock` templates, including declarations from
`native_blocks`. A crop block's generated item is its seed: add that item to
`minecraft:villager_plantable_seeds` to let farmers plant its default block state from their inventory.
Untagged items and tagged items without a block are skipped. Crop maturity follows the template's class:
beetroots mature at age 3, ordinary crops at 7; torchflower crops never reach their maximum age 2 while
still crops. Pitcher crops are not `CropBlock`s and are not harvested by this behavior. Farming still
requires a farmer at work and `mob_griefing` enabled.

## Named mob damage

Native code may use `horizon_client::mobs::DamageKind::Named(NamedDamage::new(id, tags))`
for its own damage types. The static namespaced `id` is retained in the ordinary
mob damage record, loot damage source and death-message path. Add that type's JSON
and damage-type tags to the mod's data pack too; player damage reads that registry.
Build the descriptor flags from the loaded tags again after a data-pack reload.

`NamedDamage` exposes common mob combat classifications: `IS_FIRE`,
`BYPASSES_ARMOR`, `NO_KNOCKBACK`, `BYPASSES_INVULNERABILITY`, `BYPASSES_EFFECTS`,
`BYPASSES_RESISTANCE`, `PANIC_CAUSES`, `IS_PROJECTILE`, `IS_EXPLOSION` and `IS_FALL`.
Unset flags are false. Ordinary fire immunity, Fire Resistance, armor, enchantments,
Resistance and knockback still run. The flags do not implement arbitrary additional
damage tags or change vanilla `DamageKind` variants. This compiled API needs a
matching source pin; it is not a serialized protocol enum.

## Compiled particle controllers

`ParticleEngine::controlled(name, position, translucent, controller)` creates a
named sprite particle after the normal camera-distance and particle-setting
checks. Configure its public fields and optional `set_sprite_frame`, then `push`
it into the ordinary engine queue. Missing sprite sets return `None`.

Implement `ParticleController` with owned `Clone + Debug + Send + Sync` state.
Its tick receives the current `ParticleLevel`, and returns a `ParticleUpdate`
with an optional sprite frame and extra normal particle requests. Use
`Particle::advance_age` for ordinary expiry and previous-position interpolation,
or maintain those fields in a source-specific override; `remove` ends it early.
No vanilla movement is applied to a controlled particle. Its own `random` is
separate from the engine/level generators. Choose blended rendering explicitly
for alpha-fading particles; `set_full_bright` controls light without bypassing
normal atlas, geometry or engine population limits. This is an opt-in compiled
API, not executable particle data in a resource pack.
Resource reload discards controlled particles and their pending instances, so
their numeric sprite-set handles cannot refer to a different reloaded set.

## Pinned to a Nightly

A native mod builds on one exact Horizon commit: `[compatibility] horizon_revision` in its `horizon-mixin.toml`,
and `horizon` in the `horizon-native.json` of its registry file (`.hnmod`, `horizon_mods::index::native`). Mods
pin a **Nightly's commit** (or a release's). Every Nightly ships its source and keeps its commit for good as the
tag `nightly-source/<date>-<short>` ([release-flow.md](release-flow.md#nightly-channel)), so a pinned mod still
builds after its Nightly is replaced. When the mods of one build pin different commits, the launcher says which
mod pins which and the commit the build would use, the newest of them, and builds only after the player chooses
**Build anyway** (`native-build --allow-version-mismatch`): such mods often still apply and compile. A failed build
then says the mods were made for other versions. A mod that doesn't fit the build's Horizon doesn't stop the others: when
horizon-mixins refuses it (a function it hooks, the file it adds a module to or a named hook point this Horizon
doesn't have: `E_TARGET_NOT_FOUND`, `E_MODULE`, `E_HOOK_CATALOG`; a module named like one of Horizon's or another
mod's: `E_MODULE_CONFLICT`), or the compiler's errors are in its helpers, the build runs again without it and without
the mods that need it ([dependencies](#dependencies-between-trait-mods)), at most three more compiles. The build's steps
and the Mods page say which mods were left out and why ("X was left out: made for another Horizon version", "…:
clashes with Y", "…: it needs Y, which was left out"); `native-mods.json` keeps them (`left_out`), so the build is up
to date with the same mods turned on, and a new version of a left-out mod asks for a build again. A server engine
(`--server-output`) leaves nothing out: it fails instead. Their horizon-mixins pins may differ too: the build uses the
newest of them and the launcher's own, never one older than a mod was made with. A bundle with no pin builds on
the commit of the installed version the build starts from. A pin chooses the commit even when the player picked another
version: the Mods page then says which mods pinned the commit the build compiled, and **Rebuild on Horizon**
(`native-build --ignore-pins`) builds every mod on the picked version's own commit, where a pinned mod may not
apply or compile. Its later rebuilds keep doing so.

Before a native mod is installed, the registry browser (and `horizon-launcher mods search`) says how it fits the
Horizon the selected instance plays: the commit its native build compiled, else the commit of its release or
Nightly. A mod naming hook points builds there when that commit's `NATIVE_HOOKS_API` is the one it needs or later,
else it asks for a newer Horizon; a pinned mod fits its own commit, else the line says the build moves the game to
that commit, or, when mods turned on pin another, that the build will ask for **Build anyway**. The registry's index
lists each version's `hooks` for this ([api/mod-index.md](api/mod-index.md)).

### Mods on the hook points

A mod that hooks only Horizon's native hook points (`crates/horizon-client/src/game/native_hooks.rs`, with a few
free functions in other crates: [render hook points](#render-hook-points), [items' default
components](#items-default-components), `horizon_world::loot::native_loot_table_load`, [menu slot
rules](#menu-slot-rules); empty functions the game
calls at fixed places, plus helpers, kept stable across releases) can name the version it
needs instead of pinning: `"hooks": 1` in its `.hnmod`'s `horizon-native.json`, the `NATIVE_HOOKS_API` of that
file when it was made. Hook points are only ever added: each addition raises `NATIVE_HOOKS_API`, and a mod builds
on any Horizon whose version is the one it needs or a later one. Its `horizon` is then only the commit it was tested on. The launcher builds it on the commit the other mods
pin, else on the installed version's commit (the game the player plays), after checking that commit's
`NATIVE_HOOKS_API` (read from GitHub) is at least the mod's; when it isn't and nothing pins, it builds on the commit the
mod was tested on. In its own copy of the mod it sets `horizon_revision` to the commit it builds on. Horizon's
own native mods (Zoom, FPS counter, Keystrokes, Minimap; horizon-rust/horizon-mods, `native/<name>/`) are made this way, so any of them build together
with no re-release for each Nightly.

Hook and helper authors use [API fragments](../tools/native-hook-fragments/README.md) to request a number.

The pending `hud_mode` helper fragment adds `Host::hud_mode()` and
`Host::set_hud_mode(HudMode)`. `HudMode::AllVisible` shows the HUD and permits
first-person hands; `NoHud` hides the HUD while permitting hands;
`AllHidden` hides both. Spectator, sleeping, camera and death restrictions,
and `render_hand` cancellation, still apply. `gui_render_post`'s `gui_hidden`
and `client_state().gui_hidden` report the effective HUD visibility.

A mod can keep its own three-state cycle and call `set_hud_mode` from the
existing post-handling `input_key` callback after an F1 press, or from a client
tick callback. No cancellable input hook is required. Without a mod, F1 keeps
its normal all-visible/all-hidden toggle; each F1 toggle clears the independent
hand override. The mode is window UI state, never a saved player setting.
Writes on a server or during a borrowed server guest's turn do nothing.
This fragment receives an API number when stamped after merge.

API 2 adds the item panel beside container screens and its recipe pages (`native_panel_items`,
`native_panel_recipes`, and the hook `native_panel_event` for the player's clicks, searches and keys over items), and
the game's items and recipes (`native_recipes`, `native_known_recipes`, `native_place_recipe`, `native_give`): what a
recipe viewer needs.

The pending `panel_style` helper, `native_panel_style(mod_id, style)`, lets a recipe viewer's panel look like one:
`NativePanelStyle::Viewer` draws its page buttons as square buttons on a bar over the items and its search box with the
viewer's skin, as Horizon's Recipes panel does; `NativePanelStyle::RecipeBook` (the default) keeps the recipe book's
arrows. Only the drawing changes. Bundles need `"hooks": 622`.

API 11 adds `GameLevel::native_send_particles` on authoritative servers. It sends supported
vanilla particles to nearby players. Block particles require a valid block state. Item, trail and
vibration particles are not supported by the current dispatch. Count zero sends one directional
particle. The return value says whether the call was accepted, not whether any player received it. Bundles that use it need `"hooks": 11`.

API 3 adds authoritative world lifecycle events through `native_world_lifecycle`: open ready, open failed,
and final close. Bundles that use these events need `"hooks": 3`.

===== docs/mods.md =====
# Horizon mods

Two kinds of mod run in Horizon:

| Kind | File | What it is | Where it runs |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Content mod** | `.hmod` | Data and assets: blocks, items, recipes, loot, tags, models, textures, structures. No code. | Read when the game starts |
| **Native mod** | `.hnmod` | A [horizon-mixins](https://github.com/horizon-rust/horizon-mixins) bundle: Rust that changes the game's own source before it is compiled. | In a game the launcher builds for the player ([native-mods.md](native-mods.md)) |

Fabric and NeoForge jars do **not** run as they are: their code calls and patches Minecraft's Java classes, and
Horizon has none. What carries over is the data and assets inside a jar ([modpacks.md](modpacks.md)).

Running Java mods unchanged needs a Java bridge (a JVM beside the game, proposed in [#10216](https://github.com/horizon-rust/horizon/pull/10216); not in Horizon today).
The project decided on 2026-11-09 that it will only ever be a **separate, optional download, never installed or on by
default**: when the game or the launcher finds a Java mod it may offer to turn it on, and the player decides. Before
it is promoted out of beta, its server MSPT and memory are measured against the Java client running the same mods
(plan: [#13914](https://github.com/horizon-rust/horizon/issues/13914)).

Mods are **off by default**. A fresh install has no `horizon-mods` folder and plays exactly like vanilla 26.3.

## Playing with mods

1. Install a mod from the launcher's Mods page ([mod-browser.md](mod-browser.md)) or from the game (Options > Mods >
   **Browse Mods**: Horizon's registry, checked as the launcher checks it), or put its files by hand in the
   game folder (the one holding `options.txt`): a content mod's `.hmod` (or a folder holding `horizon-mod.toml`) in
   `horizon-mods/`. Native mods go in `horizon-native-mods/`, and the launcher builds them
   ([native-mods.md](native-mods.md)).
2. Options > **Mods** lists them with what each may do ("May add blocks, items, mob effects and entity types to your
   worlds") and a switch. They start off. The choice is kept in `horizon-mods.txt` (`mod.<id>:true`).
3. A switch applies at once when the mod has only assets and data (textures, models, sounds, languages, recipes, tags,
   loot tables, advancements, functions): the game reloads its resources, then the world's data as `/reload` does,
   and the players of a world open to LAN get the new data. A mod that adds blocks, items, mob effects, entity or menu
   types, or changes world generation, still loads only when the game starts: the Mods page says to restart, and its
   switch waits outside a world. On another server's world every content mod's switch waits: the server owns its
   data ([design/mod-hotload.md](design/mod-hotload.md)).
4. A mod that can't load shows why, in red, on its row.

Two turned-on content mods never own the same namespace: the first by file name loads, and the other stays out with
the reason in the log.

Benchmarks (`--benchmark`) never load mods.

## Making a content mod

```sh
# A prebuilt binary for Linux x86_64/aarch64, macOS x86_64/arm64 or Windows x86_64 (#2120):
cargo binstall --git https://github.com/horizon-rust/horizon cargo-horizon-mod
# Or build it from source, without cargo-binstall:
cargo install --git https://github.com/horizon-rust/horizon cargo-horizon-mod
cargo horizon-mod new my-mod        # a content mod: manifest, one block with its model, loot and recipe
cd my-mod
cargo horizon-mod run               # build my-mod.hmod, then start the game with it turned on
cargo horizon-mod build             # only build: target/horizon-mod/my_mod.hmod
cargo horizon-mod check target/horizon-mod/my_mod.hmod   # the registry's checks; --json for JSON
```

- `new <path> [--name "Name"]` writes the template. `--template native [--horizon <commit>]` writes a native trait
  mod's bundle instead: `horizon-mixin.toml` (pinned to that Horizon commit, else unpinned) and an `[api]` crate with
  one hook, built with `horizon-launcher native-build <path> --trust-native-code`
  ([native-mods.md](native-mods.md#trait-mods-draft)). Any other `--template` is refused.
- `build` packs `horizon-mod.toml`, `assets/` and `data/` into `target/horizon-mod/<id>.hmod`, with the same checks
  the game makes when it reads the content. It refuses a folder with a `Cargo.toml` (code is native now) and a
  manifest without capability `registries`.
- `run` builds, then starts `horizon-client` (on `PATH`, or `--game PATH`, or `HORIZON_CLIENT`) with
  `--dev-mod target/horizon-mod/<id>.hmod`, always on. Arguments after `--` go to the game. The game reads content once
  at start, so rebuild and run again after an edit.
- `check <file>` runs what the registry runs: the file's size and SHA-256, its manifest and content, and a signature
  with `--key <public key> --signature <file>.minisig`. It reads `.hmod`, `.hnmod`, `.hpack`, resource pack `.zip`
  and `.hshader` files.
- `registry build|review|check|list|release|page` is the registry repository's CI ([api/mod-index.md](api/mod-index.md)).

### A real-game Recipes check

`python tools/lan_e2e.py --check recipes-hmod --bin-dir PATH` runs two real clients against the
host's integrated server. `PATH` must contain `horizon-client` compiled with the official native
Recipes mod and a matching `horizon-ctl`. This opt-in check fails if Recipes is absent; ordinary
LAN runs do not require it.

The runner installs the same original `tools/fixtures/recipes_hmod` package in both scratch game
folders. It finds the output of an original recipe (flint to two echo shards) in Survival and Creative.
With the recipe book empty, it verifies that missing ingredients show a warning and preserve the
occupied grid. It then fills the grid through Recipes' `+`, takes the result, and checks authoritative
inventory counts before and after Save and Quit/rejoin. Logs, screenshots and `recipes-hmod.json` go under `--out`.
As an assertion canary, changing only the fixture's result count from two to one must fail the custom
result check; restore it before running the passing case. This is a content scenario, not general
Java-mod compatibility or custom-item registration coverage.

### The manifest

```toml
id = "my_mod"                # [a-z0-9_]{1,64}: settings key, log name
name = "My mod"
version = "0.1.0"
description = "..."
authors = ["..."]
api = "0.2"                  # content mods
side = "both"
capabilities = ["registries"]
namespaces = []              # optional: more namespaces the mod's files use (below)
```

Unknown keys are refused, so a typo is an error, not a silently ignored permission.

### Content mods (API 0.2)

A content mod has capability `registries` and carries `assets/` and `data/` in the `.hmod`. It adds blocks and items
by declaring them in `data/<id>/horizon/block/*.json` and `data/<id>/horizon/item/*.json`, each copying a vanilla
template ([modpacks.md](modpacks.md#registries-02-data-driven-blocks-items-and-mob-effects)), and creative tabs, mob
effects, fluids, entity types and menu types (each below) in `creative_tab/`, `mob_effect/`, `fluid/`, `entity_type/`
and `menu/` beside them. LAN and dedicated server players play with them when they run the same mods: a client whose mods add different
content is refused when it joins, with the reason.

A server that has content mods on also checks them one by one (`horizon_protocol::mod_list`, channel
`horizon:mod_list`): in the configuration phase it sends the id, version and SHA-256 of each `.hmod` with
`side = "both"`, the client answers with its own, and a client that differs is refused with the mods named: missing,
not on this server, or another version or file. Only `side = "both"` mods are compared and sent: a `client` mod (a
HUD, a minimap) never blocks a join and is never told to a server, and a `server` mod stays private to it. A manifest
with no `side` line is `client`, so a content mod meant for both halves must say `side = "both"`, or this check
ignores it. A mod run from a folder (`--dev-mod`) has no file hash and matches by version. Native mods are not in the
list yet: their manifest has no side.

A block names its template and what differs from it:

```json
{ "template": "minecraft:iron_block",
  "properties": { "hardness": 4.0, "light": 3, "sounds": "minecraft:amethyst_block" },
  "item": true }
```

`random_ticks` makes a block tick randomly whatever its template does, as vanilla's
`isRandomlyTicking(state)` does for its own blocks, so a crop, sapling or soil on a template that never
ticks still grows: `"random_ticks": true` for every state (or `false`, to stop a template that ticks), or
`{ "property": "grown", "values": ["false"] }` for the states whose own state property (declared in
`state_properties`) is one of the values. The block's native `native_random_tick` hook then fires as it does
for a vanilla block that ticks, and its sections count it.

`state_light` selects emission by the template's or declared state properties. It has the same
`property` or ordered `properties`, `values` and optional `default` selection as a shape table;
every value is an integer from 0 to 15:

```json
{ "template": "minecraft:glass", "state_properties": { "lit": { "type": "boolean" } },
  "state_light": { "properties": ["lit"], "values": { "false": 0, "true": 15 } } }
```

Unknown properties, unreachable tuples, repeated property names and uncovered states without a
`default` reject the declaration. A selected value takes precedence over `properties.light`,
including zero. Without `state_light`, scalar light and template emission stay unchanged. The
compiler resolves the table once into the normal per-state emission facts, shared by the client,
server and saved resource cache. Light propagation uses those facts when a block changes state;
it performs no declaration lookup or mod callback.

`render_shape` says how a block is drawn whatever its template does, as vanilla's `getRenderShape`:
`"MODEL"` puts its block model in the chunk mesh, `"ENTITYBLOCK_ANIMATED"` leaves the model out for a block whose
moving parts its block entity draws every frame (a turning shaft, a machine's arm), and `"INVISIBLE"` draws nothing.
Its outline, collision, light and occlusion stay as declared: on a template that occludes (stone), the faces it
covers stay hidden unless it also sets `occludes`.

`occludes: false` makes a block occlude nothing whatever its template does, as vanilla's `noOcclusion()`: the faces
of the blocks beside it are drawn, so a thin panel or a block drawn by its block entity on a full-cube template
doesn't leave a hole in the floor under it. It is no longer a solid render either, so light passes it as it passes
leaves: dampened by 1, or not at all if its template lets skylight down. Unset or `true`, it occludes as its template
does.

A block copies its template's states. It can also declare state properties of
its own, which its template does not have — how a pipe says which sides it
connects to, or a machine whether it is running. Its states then become its
template's states times the combinations of the ones it declares, laid out as
vanilla lays out a block's states (`StateDefinition`): every property, the
template's and the declared ones, sorted by name with the first name varying
slowest, and each declared property's default value first. The order is fixed,
so ids do not move between loads, and a declared name may sort before one of
the template's (`bites` on a carved pumpkin, whose `facing` comes after it):

```json
{ "template": "minecraft:stone",
  "state_properties": {
    "facing": { "type": "enum", "values": ["north", "south", "east", "west"] },
    "lit": { "type": "boolean" } },
  "default_state": { "lit": "true" } }
```

A `boolean` is `false` and `true`, as vanilla spells them; an `enum` is a list
of values, and a name or value is lower case letters, digits and underscores.
A declared name may not be one the template already has, and `default_state`
takes one value per declared property too, so a block's default is exactly one
state of its own. Every combination has to fit in the states a world holds
(2^20), so a declaration that would not is refused when the mods load.

A block whose template is one of vanilla's signs is a sign, whatever its name: it has vanilla's sign block
entity, so the text, the editor, dye, glow ink, wax and saving all work as they do on an oak sign, and the
client draws its text. A wooden sign is two blocks and a hanging sign two more, one form per template, and the
one with the item names its wall form:

```json
{ "template": "minecraft:oak_sign", "wood_type": "ruby:elm", "wall_block": "ruby:elm_wall_sign" }
```

```json
{ "template": "minecraft:oak_wall_sign", "wood_type": "ruby:elm", "item": false }
===== docs/native-client.md =====
# Horizon native client

`horizon-client` is the playable Rust client for Minecraft Java Edition 26.3.
It streams a world around the player on worker threads, renders every vanilla
block model with smooth lighting and biome tints, and runs the validated player
physics, health and hunger at 20 ticks per second. You can mine with vanilla
hardness/tool rules, collect drops from the block loot tables, place blocks,
craft in the 2x2 inventory grid or on a crafting table, and save and reload worlds
in vanilla's Anvil format.

**Status:** playable; players get it through [the launcher](../launcher/README.md). The [harness table](status.md#what-the-harnesses-compare)
in the README scores each subsystem against vanilla and lists what is still
missing; [Rust rewrite status](rust-parity.md) has the details.

On Windows, the launcher uses a stable executable path for each game home across
release and native-mod updates. Its private runtime copy lives under the launcher's
data folder in `run/<home key>/game/`; installed versions remain intact. Opening a
world to other machines can still require an initial Windows Firewall permission.
The launcher does not create firewall rules or change network-profile settings.

## musl Linux: manual installation

On x86-64 musl distributions, download `horizon-linux-musl-x86_64.tar.gz` from the
[Nightly release](https://github.com/horizon-rust/horizon/releases/tag/nightly), when available. This optional
build is omitted if its build fails. The launcher does not install or update it. Its desktop gameplay and
vanilla parity are still unverified; see the [musl build limitations](release-flow.md#builds).

You need a working X11 or Wayland desktop, musl, libgcc, ALSA and libudev. For Vulkan, install both the loader
(`libvulkan.so.1`) and your GPU's driver. On Alpine, the common runtime packages are:

```sh
sudo apk add musl libgcc alsa-lib eudev-libs vulkan-loader
```

Install the appropriate Mesa/Vulkan driver and window-system libraries for your desktop separately; OpenGL
also needs a working musl-compatible OpenGL/EGL stack. NVIDIA's proprietary userspace drivers require glibc
and cannot be loaded by this build; see [Alpine's GPU requirements](https://wiki.alpinelinux.org/wiki/Requirements).

Resource preparation needs a **musl-compatible system JDK 25** (including `java` and `javac`). Mojang's bundled
Linux Java runtime needs glibc. Install JDK 25 from your distribution or another musl-compatible provider,
set `JAVA_HOME` to that JDK, and check `"$JAVA_HOME/bin/java" -version`. You also need your own Minecraft Java
Edition 26.3 installation and its server jar. Download the server jar yourself from the `downloads.server.url`
in that version's launcher JSON. Horizon's preparer does not download it.

Extract the archive and, inside its folder, run the following with your actual paths:

```sh
./horizon-prepare --prepare --minecraft-dir /path/to/minecraft --server-jar /path/to/server.jar
./horizon-prepare sounds --minecraft-dir /path/to/minecraft
./horizon-client --resources "$PWD/.local/horizon-client/26.3"
```

The sounds command copies the sounds from your Minecraft installation's asset store. If that store is
incomplete, obtain the missing assets through your Minecraft launcher first. Preparation details and alternative
layouts are described below.

Updates are manual: download and extract the next musl archive, prepare its resources, and use that version's
programs. Keep your saves in a separate `HORIZON_HOME` and point each version at it. Back up saves before
updating. The bundled `horizon-server` also refuses automatic updates because the server update archives use
glibc. An absent musl archive is not a reason to install the glibc archive on a musl system.

## Run from a checkout

Use current stable Rust (1.90+) and JDK 25 (Python 3.11+ only for the development
harnesses). A checkout never
downloads the game: resource preparation imports **your own copy** (the 26.3
client from your official launcher install, and the 26.3 server jar you
downloaded yourself; see [Build from source](#run-from-a-checkout)),
verifies it against the pinned checksums, runs vanilla's data generator and small
exporters against those jars, and extracts the jar's assets and data into ignored
`.local/horizon-client/26.3/`. Sounds (about 380 MB) are copied from your
launcher's asset store by `horizon-prepare sounds`, each file checked against the asset
index's SHA-1. Preparation also tries to replace the JAR's gray 1×1 title panorama
placeholders with the full textures from that asset store. If the store is missing or
incomplete, preparation logs a warning and keeps the placeholders. To import the panorama
later without rerunning the Java exporters, run `horizon-prepare panorama` (or
`cargo run --release -p horizon-prepare -- panorama` in a checkout). Pass
`--minecraft-dir PATH` when using a different Minecraft installation.
Prepared resources are private; never commit them.

Preparation is `horizon-prepare` (`crates/horizon-prepare`, Rust): it needs no Python, only
the JDK, for the small exporters that call Mojang's own code. Release folders ship it beside
`horizon-client`, and the launcher prepares with it and copies the sounds as part of preparing.

`--output DIR` puts the resources elsewhere (the exporters' scratch files stay in the version
folder's `.local/`), for preparing and for the asset commands alike. `horizon-prepare --check`
exits 0 when the resources are complete and current for this preparer, and otherwise prints
each gap with the command that fills it on stdout and exits 1. It also exits 1 when it can't
check, with `error:` on stderr (no `research-lock.json`, say). It runs no Java and reads no game
files: it tests which files are there and the versions they were made for, not their contents, so
a launcher that suspects damage prepares again. A preparation stamps its resources with the
preparer's `OUTPUT_VERSION` (`horizon-prepare.json`) when it finishes, and removes the stamp
before it writes anything, so `--check` reports a folder being prepared, or whose preparation
stopped, as not current. Resources prepared before the stamp existed report an unknown version
until they are prepared again. One preparation at a time writes to a folder: each holds
`.horizon-prepare.lock` in the resources and in the version folder's `.local/`, and a second one
waits for the first. `--complete` prepares, copies the sounds, and fails unless `--check` would
then pass: one command for resources with nothing missing. `--progress json` prints one JSON
object a line instead of plain text (its format is part of the Launch API:
[docs/api/launch.md](api/launch.md#--progress-json)). Exit codes: 0 done, 1 failed or gaps found,
2 a refused command line.

A launcher whose files are laid out its own way names them one by one instead of
`--minecraft-dir`: `--version-json` (the pinned version's JSON), `--client-jar`,
`--libraries-dir` (the client's libraries, Maven layout) and `--assets-dir` (the asset store,
with `indexes/` and `objects/`); each replaces only what it names, and every file is still
checked against `research-lock.json`'s pins. When an earlier import beside `--root` is still
verified, it is reused and the named files aren't read. Two things no such launcher keeps:
- **The server jar.** The first `--prepare` needs `--server-jar`. Horizon never downloads it; a
  launcher downloads it (the version JSON's `downloads.server`) only for a signed-in account that
  owns Minecraft: Java Edition, as `horizon-launcher` does.
- **Java 25.** `--java-home PATH` names a runtime the launcher manages. The preparer takes the first
  Java of the pinned version from `JAVA_HOME`, the `PATH`, `--java-home`, then the official
  launcher's runtimes.

```sh
cargo run --release -p horizon-prepare -- --prepare --server-jar ~/Downloads/server.jar
cargo run --release -p horizon-prepare -- sounds
cargo run --release -p horizon-client -- --render-distance 12
```

Development builds that link quickly: `cargo build --profile fast -p horizon-client`.

`HORIZON_OCCLUSION_PROFILE=1` enables detailed entity-occlusion phase and bounds-scan timings
in `horizon-ctl stats` and `bench`. The normal benchmark keeps these CPU-clock reads disabled;
query, cache-hit and cull counters remain available without profiling.

Optional Windows allocator build features are documented in
[Windows allocator experiments](windows-allocators.md).

| Option | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `--world NAME` | World folder under `saves/` (default "New World"); reopened if it exists |
| `--seed N` | Seed for a new world (numbers or text, like vanilla) |
| `--creative` | Creative mode (instant breaking, pick block) |
| `--render-distance N` | Chunks, 2..128 (default 12) |
| `--resources PATH` | Prepared resource directory |

| Control | Action |
| --- | --- |
| Mouse | Look (vanilla default sensitivity) |
| WASD / Space / Left Shift / Left Ctrl | Move, jump, sneak, sprint |
| Left / right click | Break (hold) / place or use; right click opens crafting tables |
| Middle click | Pick block (creative) |
| 1–9, scroll | Select hotbar slot |
| E | Inventory and 2x2 crafting (shift-click to quick-move or craft all) |
| Q / Ctrl+Q | Drop one / drop stack |
| F3 | Debug overlay (position, chunk, light, biome, FPS) |
| F3 + S | Dump the texture atlas to `screenshots/debug/`: `minecraft_textures_atlas_gui.png` with `minecraft_textures_atlas_gui.txt` beside it, every sprite's rect in texels (vanilla's `TextureManager.dumpAllSheets`) |
| Esc | Pause menu (Save and Quit) |

Worlds autosave every 6000 ticks (5 minutes), when chunks unload and on quit.
On headless Linux use `vscreen start ./target/release/horizon-client` and
`vscreen shot`.

Sound: the game prints the output it opened (`Horizon: audio output ...`) or
why it has none (`Horizon: no sound: ...`). It opens none with
`HORIZON_NO_AUDIO` set, nor on a `vscreen`, where tests run beside someone
listening; `HORIZON_AUDIO=1` opens it there anyway, so send it to a null sink
(`PIPEWIRE_PROPS='{ target.object=<sink> node.dont-fallback=true }'`). Don't
mute a test run's stream in the mixer: PipeWire remembers the mute for every
later `horizon-client`. The launcher starts the game as "Horizon" for that
reason. On Linux, Horizon requests a 1024-frame fixed buffer for lower latency;
if your audio server underruns (e.g. PipeWire with `min-quantum` ≥ 1024), set
`HORIZON_AUDIO_BUFFER=2048` or `HORIZON_AUDIO_BUFFER=default` to use the backend's
default buffer size.

### Experimental older servers

The native Rust adapter recognizes 26.2, 26.1.x, 1.21.11, 1.21.9/1.21.10,
1.21.7/1.21.8, 1.21.6, 1.21.5, 1.21.4, 1.21.2/1.21.3, 1.21/1.21.1,
1.20.5/1.20.6, 1.20.3/1.20.4 and 1.20.2. It is off by default while compatibility is incomplete. Set
`HORIZON_OLDER_SERVERS=1` before starting the game; the server's status ping selects
the adapter. The client still needs its prepared **26.3** resources.

```sh
HORIZON_OLDER_SERVERS=1 cargo run --profile fast -p horizon-client -- --server 127.0.0.1:25565
```

In PowerShell, set `$env:HORIZON_OLDER_SERVERS = '1'`, then run the same Cargo
command without the environment assignment at its start.

This does not load Fabric JARs or emulate every older version's movement and
combat. Older item components still have gaps; a recognized protocol is not a
promise of complete compatibility. The 1.21.6 and 1.21.7/1.21.8 adapters use vanilla
packet layouts shared by these releases, with separate item/sound mappings for
1.21.6. The 1.21.5 adapter also removes vanilla's chunk-buffer padding and converts crouch/action commands,
hanging-entity metadata and the pre-1.21.6 attribute-modifier/equippable layouts.
The 1.21.4 bridge converts its older chunks, tooltip flags, nested stacks, chat,
team rules and entity metadata. Old inventory clicks use the server's authoritative
slot/cursor resynchronization. Smithing previews follow the server's synced trim
registry; inline 1.21.4 trim holders and templates outside the known vanilla item
set remain unsupported.
The 1.21.2/1.21.3 bridge also handles particle flags and client-chosen trail lifetimes,
vehicle movement, tab-list hats, integer custom-model data, old creaking-heart states
and the transient creaking type. Custom-model data uses the first float when sent
back; integers beyond exact float precision can lose precision. Server-delegated
block/entity picking is not supported on 1.20.2 through 1.21.3, whose old pick packet
requires a client inventory index instead of a world target.
The 1.21/1.21.1 bridge converts older recipe declarations and book updates, food
