1-# Native mods
2-
3-`native_explosion_entities(dimension, center, entities)` (server) runs after `native_explosion_detonate`, before an explosion at `center` hurts and pushes anything: `entities` holds the ids of the entities in its reach (`ServerExplosion.hurtEntities`), players aside, which `native_living_incoming_damage` hears. Take out the ids to spare: they are neither hurt nor pushed, as NeoForge's `ExplosionEvent.Detonate` does with its entity list. Bundles need "hooks": 644.
4-
5:`native_chat_suggestion_applied(original, start, end, suggestion, input)` reports a suggestion accepted in the open chat by Tab or a mouse click, after the text and caret have changed. `original` is the suggestion list's input before any completion; `start` and `end` are the replaced range in that string, in Java UTF-16 units (end exclusive). They keep referring to `original` when Tab cycles through the list. `suggestion` is the selected replacement text, and `input` is the actual resulting field value, including any length-limit truncation. This is a read-only notification: previews, list navigation, server replies, ordinary typing and command-block editors do not fire it. Trait mods implement `Hooks::chat_suggestion_applied`; mixin bundles use `chat_suggestion_applied`, hook version 1. Bundles need "hooks": 566 (assigned at merge).
6-
7-API 362 adds `native_compute_fov_modifier(player, base, modifier)` on the client each tick for the local player's FOV scale, after vanilla effects and before zoom and camera easing. `player` contains the calculation inputs and `base` is the original scale. Non-finite modifiers or values outside 0.01..=10 fall back to `base`. It does not affect a spectator camera on a mob. Bundles need "hooks": 362.
8-
9-The client hook `native_client_packet(packet)` hears each play packet the server sends, decoded (`ClientboundPlayPacket`, `horizon_protocol`'s `play::Clientbound`), just before the game handles it, custom payloads included and in the order they came. It only reads: the game handles the packet as it would without the mod. Packets the network thread handles itself (keep-alives, bundle delimiters, resource packs, cookies, server links, transfers, disconnects, the configuration switch, `minecraft:brand`, far terrain tiles) and the configuration phase are not heard. Bundles need "hooks": 408.
10-
11-`native_registry_name(registry, id)` (`host.registry_name`) is the name of a protocol id in a registry: `minecraft:item`, `minecraft:data_component_type`, `minecraft:entity_type`, `minecraft:sound_event`, `minecraft:particle_type` and the like. It names what `native_client_packet`'s packets carry as numbers (a `Stack`'s item and components, `AddEntity`'s type, `Sound`, `LevelParticles`). On a client the ids are those of the server it is connected to, so a registry the server synced (enchantments, painting variants) and a mod's own data components, when the server carries them, name too; on a server they are the ids it sends. It answers `None` for an unknown registry, an id out of range or negative, and while no server is connected. Bundles need `"hooks": 637`.
12-
13-The client hook `native_render_name_tag(entity_id, kind, vanilla_visible, visible, text)` runs at player and mob, armor stand, and item frame name decisions before shared geometry. Set `visible` to override vanilla and `text` to `Some` literal replacement text (NeoForge `RenderNameTagEvent`). Distance and entity availability still apply; the local player's tag is considered only when its own camera and settings gates allow it. Bundles need "hooks": 372.
14-
15-Native mods are **mixin bundles** that change the game's own Rust source before it is compiled, with
16-[horizon-mixins](https://github.com/horizon-rust/horizon-mixins) (MIT, by levzzz5154, made for Horizon). A bundle
17-is a `horizon-mixin.toml` and a few `.rs` helpers that hook into named functions of the game (HEAD, BEFORE_CALL,
18-RETURN, MODIFY_ARG, OVERWRITE...; the tool's README lists them). A port such as Veinminer is three HEAD hooks and
19-one module. Start a mod from [horizon-native-mod-template](https://github.com/horizon-rust/horizon-native-mod-template).
20-
21-Native mods are native code with the player's rights, not sandboxed. They are always on in the launcher: there is no
22-switch. The first install shows a trust notice, and every build waits behind a red warning; the registry lists one only
23-after a review (its later releases are checked by machine); and they play in a game of their own, built for the
24-instance they are installed in.
25-
26-## Compilation cache
27-
28-Native builds share `native/target/fast` (Performance and Dev) and `native/target/release`.
29-After a build finishes, the launcher clears these compiler outputs if their combined size exceeds
30-30 GiB. The installed game or exported server stays usable; a later changed build recompiles.
31-`horizon-launcher settings native-cache-gib N` changes the limit (`default` restores 30; `0`
32-disables automatic cleanup). This is an after-build limit, not a cap on peak compilation space.
33-
34-`horizon-launcher native-cache` reports its path, size in bytes and limit as JSON.
35-`horizon-launcher native-cache --clear` frees the compilation cache immediately when builds are idle.
36-Both use the same process lock as native builds; cleanup also refuses busy Cargo profile locks.
37-It preserves lock files, refuses symbolic links and Windows junctions, and never targets installed
38-versions, exported servers, instances, saves, mods, Rust toolchains or downloaded crates.
39-
40-
41-## Conditional item model properties
42-
43-A client mod supplies its own namespaced `minecraft:condition` property by implementing
44-`Hooks::item_model_property`. This hook is the registration: match your property name, answer
45-through `value`, and leave other names untouched. The resolver calls it when choosing a branch,
46-not when loading resources. It defaults to false without a provider and skips dispatch entirely
47-when no trait mods are loaded. Vanilla properties do not enter the hook.
48-
49-```rust,ignore
50-fn item_model_property(
51-    &self,
52-    _host: &dyn Host,
53-    event: &mut ItemModelPropertyEvent<'_>,
54-) {
55-    if event.property == "example:charged" {
56-        event.value = event.stack.patch.get("example:charged").and_then(Option::as_ref)
57-            .and_then(serde_json::Value::as_bool) == Some(true);
58-    }
59-}
60-```
61-
62-`Host`, `ItemModelPropertyEvent`, and `StackView` are exported by `horizon_client::mod_api`. The stack is borrowed,
63-including for nested bundle items: `item`, `count`, explicit component `patch` (not merged
64-defaults), and the runtime bundle selection. Listeners run in mod order; later answers win.
65-Use `"property": "example:charged"` with both `on_true` and `on_false` in the item's
--
680-
681-### Recipe book category declarations (first slice of #5501)
682-
683-The pending `recipe_book_categories` API fragment adds a declaration hook and a
684-lookup helper. It has no API number until stamping; do not require a guessed
685:hooks version. Trait mods import `RecipeBookCategory` from `horizon_client::mod_api`
686-and append declarations in `Hooks::recipe_book_categories`:
687-
688-```rust,ignore
689-event.categories.push(RecipeBookCategory {
690-    id: "mymod:crushing".into(),
691-    recipe_type: "mymod:crusher".into(),
692-    icon: "mymod:crusher".into(),
693-});
694-```
695-
696-Mixin bundles use `native_recipe_book_categories(categories)` at the same hook
697-point. `host.recipe_book_category("mymod:crusher")` (the mixin helper is
698-`native_recipe_book_category`) returns the validated declaration, including its
699-icon, or `None`. It does not grant recipes. Declarations are requested lazily at
700-first lookup, cached for that recipe table, and invalidated on a new session,
701-server recipe-content replacement or server `/reload`. No lookup means no hook
702-call or per-frame work. A client validates against its own custom recipes unless
703-`native_datapack_sync` has supplied the server's type (API 120).
704-
705-Every field must be a fully namespaced resource id. Category ids and recipe types
706-must be outside `minecraft:`; the type must occur in the current custom recipe
707-table. The icon must name a registered, non-air item. Invalid ids, unknown types,
708-missing/unknown icons, and duplicate category ids or recipe types are logged and
709-skipped, never panicked on. The first valid declaration wins each id and type;
710-an invalid declaration does not reserve either. This slice supports one category
711-per type, not per-recipe category selectors.
712-
713-**Not yet book UI support:** custom recipes still have no book displays or
714-unlock/display-id synchronization. This slice deliberately leaves vanilla tabs,
715-search, layouts and unlock behavior unchanged. Adding custom tabs, displaying and
716-searching recipes there, and unlocking them like vanilla remain follow-up work
717-for #5501; a declaration alone will not make a recipe visible.
718-
719-### A mod's own ingredient kinds
720-
721-A recipe's ingredient that names a type in a namespace outside `minecraft:` reaches
722-`native_ingredient(kind, data, items)` at load time. Fill `items` with the item ids the
723-ingredient matches (item ids only: components are not checked; a match-time hook follows
724-separately). Duplicates are removed keeping first-seen order. The hook may be called more
725-than once per recipe; the answer must be the same. An unclaimed kind leaves the list empty
726-and the recipe fails, as an unknown kind does.
727-
728-### Data files
729-
730-A bundle's `data/` folder (any files) is staged as `native-data/<mod>/` at the root of the workspace. A helper or
731-module compiles a file in with `horizon_client::native_data!("<mod id>", "path/in/data")` (`&'static [u8; N]`,
732-`crate::native_data!` inside the lib), since hook points API 41. A mod's crate keeps its own data in its folder and
733-reads it with `include_bytes!(concat!(env!("CARGO_MANIFEST_DIR"), "/data/…"))`. The registry's `.hnmod` may carry
734-both (`mixins/<id>/data/…`, `mixins/<id>/<crate>/data/…`).
735-
736-Both are compiled in: neither folder is beside the game when it runs. A file the game reads at run time is one a
737-post-build step writes into `HORIZON_NATIVE_DATA_DIR` (below), copying it from `HORIZON_NATIVE_BUNDLE_DATA_DIR` or
738-making it; the game finds it with `native_built_data("<mod>")` (None only when the game can't tell its own folder).
739-
740-**What a `.hnmod` holds.** Only files under `mixins/<id>/`: `.rs` and `.toml` files, the data folders above, and the
741-text files a crate folder carries (`README*`, `LICENSE*`, `COPYING*`, `NOTICE*`, `.gitignore`, in the bundle's
742-folder or a crate's), so a crate that compiles its README in builds the same from the registry as from a folder. At
743-most 512 files and 64 MiB unpacked; anything else refuses the bundle. A local folder build has no such limits.
744-
745-**One `.hmod` for content and code.** A mod's content and its native code ship as one `.hmod`, and what is inside
--
5587-- Client and server each apply their own mods, so both need the mod. A client without it (a vanilla Java client, or
5588-  Horizon without the mod) fills in vanilla's defaults for the same stacks: a stone stack the server caps at 16 still
5589-  looks like it stacks to 64 there.
5590-- The creative search tab matches vanilla's exported tooltip text, so a changed `item_name` is not searchable yet.
